---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../../../../data/topics.json';
import kidData from '../../../../data/kid-summaries.json';

const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '';
const { subjects, topics } = data;
const topic = topics.find((t) => t.slug === 'subjects/math/foundational/linear-functions');
const kidSummary = kidData[topic.slug] ?? null;
const subject = subjects.find((s) => s.id === topic.subject);
const seq = topics.filter((t) => t.status !== 'planned');
const idx = seq.indexOf(topic);
const prev = seq[(idx - 1 + seq.length) % seq.length];
const next = seq[(idx + 1) % seq.length];

const lineCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: 'm*x + c', label: 'y = m·x + c', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.4 }],
  params: [
    { name: 'm', label: 'slope m', min: -5, max: 5, step: 0.1, value: 1 },
    { name: 'c', label: 'intercept c', min: -8, max: 8, step: 0.5, value: 0 },
  ],
  x: { min: -10, max: 10, label: 'x' },
  y: { min: -10, max: 10, label: 'y' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 380,
};

const slopeCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'slope = rise / run', note: 'How far up for how far along — the steepness of the line.' },
    { math: 'm = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁)', note: 'Between any two points of the line, this ratio is the same. That sameness is what makes it a line.' },
    { math: 'y − y₁ = m·(x − x₁)', note: 'Anchor the line at one known point and let m carry the direction.' },
    { math: 'y = m·x + c', note: 'Rearranged, with c the y-value where the line crosses the axis.' },
  ],
};

const perpCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'm*x', label: 'y = m·x', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '0 - x/m', label: 'y = −x/m', color: '#b42318', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'm', label: 'slope m', min: 0.5, max: 4, step: 0.25, value: 2 },
  ],
  x: { min: -10, max: 10, label: 'x' },
  y: { min: -10, max: 10, label: 'y' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};
---
<Layout title={`${topic.title} — ${subject.name} — Interactive Atlas`} description={topic.summary}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topic-grid">
      <aside class="topic-aside">
        <a class="back" href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>← all of {subject.name}</a>
        <dl>
          <dt>subject</dt><dd><span class="dot" style={`--dot:${subject.ink}; display:inline-block; margin-right:.4rem`}></span>{subject.name}</dd>
          <dt>level</dt><dd>{topic.level}</dd>
          <dt>time</dt><dd>{topic.minutes} min</dd>
          <dt>tags</dt><dd>{topic.tags.slice(0, 3).join(', ')}</dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>
      <article>
        <header class="topic-head">
          <p class="crumb"><a href={B + '/'}>atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span><a href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>{subject.name}</a><span class="sep">/</span>{topic.level}</p>
          <h1>{topic.title}</h1>
          <p class="lede">{topic.summary}</p>
        </header>

        {kidSummary && (
          <aside class="kid-note" aria-label="Summary for young readers">
            <p><strong>For young explorers:</strong> {kidSummary}</p>
          </aside>
        )}
        <div class="topic-body">
          <h2>The idea</h2>
          <p>
            A linear function is a promise: <em>equal steps in x buy equal steps in y, always.</em>
            Speed up the x-axis by one and the y-axis responds by exactly the same amount every
            time, forever. That single property is why lines are the workhorses of mathematics —
            they are the only graphs whose future you can predict from any two moments of their
            past. Two numbers seal the promise: <code>m</code>, how steep, and <code>c</code>,
            where it starts. Drag them below and watch the line keep the deal.
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 1</span><span>slope is steepness, intercept is starting point — drag both</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="plotter" data-config={JSON.stringify(lineCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <p>
            The form <code>y = m·x + c</code> is worth internalising with real examples. Water
            freezes at 0 °C and boils at 100 °C, corresponding to 32 °F and 212 °F — so the
            conversion is linear: F = 1.8·C + 32. The 1.8 is the slope (nine Fahrenheit degrees
            per five Celsius degrees) and the 32 is the intercept (where Fahrenheit's scale
            happens to put zero). A taxi fare is linear in kilometres: a base charge plus a
            per-kilometre rate. A mobile plan is linear in gigabytes used. Whenever you meet a
            real relationship, the first instinct of a mathematician is: <em>is it linear? and if
            not, over how small a stretch can I treat it as linear?</em>
          </p>

          <h2>Gradient: measuring the rate</h2>
          <p>
            The <strong>gradient</strong> of a line is its steepness measured honestly:
            <code>m = rise ÷ run</code> — how far the line climbs for each step you take along.
            Pick any two points on it, say (1, 3) and (4, 12): the rise is 12 − 3 = 9, the run is
            4 − 1 = 3, so m = 9 ÷ 3 = 3. Now the crucial property: pick any <em>other</em> pair
            of points and you get the same 3. On a curve this ratio changes as you move along;
            the fact that it does <em>not</em> change is exactly what makes the graph a straight
            line. Gradient always carries units when the axes have them — a distance–time line
            with gradient 3 has units of metres per second, and the gradient <em>is</em> the
            speed. A negative gradient slopes downhill: cooling coffee loses 2 °C per minute, so
            m = −2. A horizontal line has m = 0 (nothing rises); a vertical line has undefined
            gradient (run = 0, and dividing by zero is not allowed — which is itself a lesson).
          </p>

          <h2>Building the equation from points</h2>
          <p>
            Given any two points, the entire line follows in two moves, because the slope fixes
            the direction and a point fixes the position:
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 2</span><span>from two points to y = m·x + c</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="equationStep" data-config={JSON.stringify(slopeCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <p>
            A worked example with ugly-but-honest numbers: the line through (2, 1) and (6, 9).
            Gradient m = (9 − 1) ÷ (6 − 2) = 2. Anchor at (2, 1): y − 1 = 2·(x − 2), which
            rearranges to <code>y = 2x − 3</code>. Check with the second point: 2 × 6 − 3 = 9 ✓.
            The check matters — one arithmetic slip and you own the wrong line with total
            confidence. Special cases come free: a line through the origin has c = 0, which is
            <em>direct proportion</em> (double the input, double the output); a horizontal line
            is just y = c, a constant function. And notice the intercept's meaning: c is the
            y-value where the line crosses the y-axis — the taxi's base fare, the starting
            temperature, the "flag falls" value of whatever story the graph tells.
          </p>

          <h2>Drawing lines and reading practical graphs</h2>
          <p>
            To draw y = m·x + c by hand, a table of three values suffices (two determine the
            line; the third is the mistake-catcher). The slicker method: plot the intercept c on
            the y-axis, then use the gradient as a set of walking directions — from there, rise
            m and run 1, mark, connect. For y = −½x + 4: start at (0, 4), step right 1 and down
            ½ repeatedly, and the line draws itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            Practical graphs ("graphs in real situations") are where this earns its keep, and
            the habit is always the same: <em>gradient = rate, intercept = starting value</em>.
            A journey graph's gradient is speed; a filling-tank graph's gradient is flow rate; a
            mobile-bill graph's gradient is the price per gigabyte. Worked example: a taxi
            charges a £3 base fare plus £1.20 per kilometre, so fare = 3 + 1.20·km. The line
            crosses the vertical axis at 3 (you pay before moving); its gradient 1.20 is the
            per-km rate; and reading the graph backwards answers questions directly — at £15 the
            horizontal line meets the fare line at 10 km. Real bills are often only
            <em>piecewise</em>-linear (a cheaper rate beyond 10 km bends the line), and the bend
            points are where the interesting questions live.
          </p>

          <h2>Parallel and perpendicular lines</h2>
          <p>
            Two lines are <strong>parallel</strong> exactly when they share a gradient: y = 2x +
            1 and y = 2x − 3 rise identically and can never meet, no matter how far you extend
            them — that is all "parallel" means, and it is why equal gradients are the test used
            in coordinate proofs everywhere. <strong>Perpendicular</strong> lines hide a
            subtler gem: their gradients multiply to −1. The perpendicular partner of m = 2 is
            m = −½; of m = 3, m = −⅓. Why? A perpendicular turns the direction of the line
            through 90°, which swaps the rise and run and flips a sign: rise 2 for run 1
            becomes rise −1 for run 2. Drag the slider below and watch the pair pivot together,
            always crossing at right angles:
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 3</span><span>perpendicular partners — slope m and −1/m always cross at 90°</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="plotter" data-config={JSON.stringify(perpCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <p>
            Put the rule to work: find the line through (3, 4) perpendicular to y = ½x + 2. The
            perpendicular gradient is −2, so y − 4 = −2·(x − 3), giving <code>y = −2x + 10</code>.
            This construction — drop a perpendicular from a point to a line — is how you compute
            the shortest distance from a point to a line, the mathematics behind "how far is the
            ship from the shipping lane" and every nearest-neighbour search in computing.
          </p>

          <h2>Length and midpoint on the grid</h2>
          <p>
            Coordinates also let you measure. The distance between two points comes from
            Pythagoras: the horizontal gap and vertical gap form the legs of a right triangle,
            and the segment is its hypotenuse, so <code>d = √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²)</code>. From
            (1, 2) to (4, 6): the gaps are 3 and 4, so d = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5 — the 3-4-5
            triangle, rediscovered on graph paper. The <strong>midpoint</strong> is the average
            of the coordinates: ((1+4)/2, (2+6)/2) = (2.5, 4), which makes sense because
            halfway along a segment means half of each offset. Together, length + gradient +
            midpoint give you full forensic power over any figure drawn in coordinates: prove a
            shape is a parallelogram (opposite sides equal length and same gradient), a rhombus,
            or a right angle (perpendicular gradients) — coordinate geometry is Euclid rebuilt
            on arithmetic.
          </p>

          <h2>A full modelling problem: the break-even point</h2>
  <p>
    Two lines crossing is also the shape of every business plan. Suppose you print tote
    bags: a £500 printing rig plus £9 of materials per bag, and each bag sells for £15.
    Cost is linear in bags made: <code>C = 500 + 9x</code> (intercept 500 — the fixed cost
    you pay before making anything; slope 9 — the per-bag rate). Revenue is linear too:
    <code>R = 15x</code>, straight through the origin (sell zero, earn zero — direct
    proportion). Profit is the gap, and the <strong>break-even point</strong> is where the
    lines cross: 15x = 500 + 9x, so 6x = 500, x ≈ 83.3 — the 84th bag is the first one after
    which you are genuinely profitable. Below it, the business is a subsidy; above it, every
    bag adds £6. Read the geometry honestly and you can answer follow-ups without new maths:
    raise the selling price to £18 and the crossing slides left (a steeper revenue line meets
    cost sooner); negotiate materials to £8 and the cost line flattens, also moving break-even
    down. Same two-line diagram, every decision in the model — which is why "linear model" is
    the first phrase in every business analytics course.
  </p>
  <p>
    The general lesson generalises: whenever two linear processes compete — cost vs revenue,
    distance covered vs distance remaining, money in savings vs money owed — their
    <em>difference</em> is itself linear, and solving for zero finds the moment of
    handshake. That is all an equation like <code>m₁x + c₁ = m₂x + c₂</code> ever is: two
    stories agreeing on one number.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading between the lines: inequalities on the graph</h2>
  <p>
    The line's last gift is that it divides the plane in two. Every linear inequality —
    <code>y &gt; 2x − 3</code>, say — is a <em>region</em>: draw the line (dashed for strict
    inequality, solid for ≤ or ≥), pick a test point not on it, say the origin, and check
    whether it satisfies the rule (0 &gt; −3 ✓). The origin's side of the line is the answer;
    shade accordingly. "Systems" of them just intersect the regions — which is exactly how
    linear programming, the mathematics of every "maximise profit subject to budget, hours
    and materials" problem, begins: shade the feasible region, then slide the profit line
    until it last touches the region's corner. The exam version of the same idea is
    graphing regions and testing points, and the everyday version is every budget you have
    ever stayed inside without noticing it was linear algebra.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
          <p>
            Two linear equations solved together — <em>simultaneous equations</em> — are
            secretly a question about lines: where do y = 2x + 1 and y = −x + 7 cross? Set them
            equal: 2x + 1 = −x + 7, so 3x = 6, x = 2, and y = 5. The algebra is the geometry;
            parallel lines (equal gradients) give the nonsense 1 = −3, which is the algebraic
            way of saying "never meet". Extend to three equations and you are doing linear
            algebra, the machinery behind Google's ranking, computer graphics and every
            spreadsheet solver.
          </p>
          <p>
            Linearity is also the backbone of statistics: scatter your data and draw the best
            straight line through it, and you have linear regression — the most used model in
            science, and the reason "correlation" comes with a slope and an intercept at all.
            The honest caveat is that it finds the best <em>linear</em> story, not the true one:
            fit a line to an exponential curve and you will be confidently wrong. Physicists
            court linearity deliberately — Ohm's law (V = I·R), Hooke's law (F = k·x), the
            ideal-gas law at fixed volume — because every law is linear in a small enough
            neighbourhood of any point. The calculus waiting in later chapters makes that
            precise: a curve, magnified far enough, <em>is</em> a line, and the gradient of
            that local line is the derivative. The linear function is not just chapter one;
            it is the microscope through which every other chapter is read.
          </p>

          <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Mathematics 0580 syllabus, sections E2.9 and E3.2–E3.7; OpenStax, <em>College Algebra</em>, chapter 4; Strang, <em>Introduction to Linear Algebra</em>.</p>
        </div>

        <nav class="topic-next" aria-label="Lesson navigation">
          <div><span class="tn-label">previous lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${prev.slug}/`}>{prev.title}</a></div>
          <div style="text-align:right"><span class="tn-label">next lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${next.slug}/`}>{next.title}</a></div>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
